<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Technology – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #333; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Tech-Free or Tech Bound?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain a routine task. Interactive: Negotiate solutions to challenges in group work.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain routine tech-free activities by responding to peer questions (e.g., "I like to read when I'm not using my phone."). Can ask and answer questions about daily tech-free habits (e.g., "I can read more when I am not using my computer."). Can negotiate solutions to group challenges related to technology use by asking and responding to questions (e.g., "How can we reduce screen time?" "We could set tech-free hours.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by showing images of various activities, some tech-free (e.g., reading a physical book, playing board games, gardening) and some tech-bound (e.g., scrolling social media, playing video games, watching videos). Students raise their hands if they *often* do the tech-free activity or if they *usually* do the tech-bound one. Teacher asks "Which one do you find more *meaningful*?" or "Which activity helps you feel more *relaxed*?" encouraging brief responses. This activates prior vocabulary like "technology," "cellphone," "social media," "disconnect," "interact," "relaxed," "focused."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using modals and comparatives related to the activities. For example: "I *can* read a book and feel more *focused*. It's *easier* for me to relax without my *device*." "We *could* spend more time outdoors. I think it is *more difficult* to *disconnect* when my *cellphone* is next to me." The teacher highlights the pronunciation of /θ/ and /ð/ sounds in words like "think," "with," "mouth" by slowly and clearly articulating them, using a minimal pair like "thigh" vs. "thy" for emphasis. Students repeat chorally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something is 'easier,' is it simple or complicated?" (Expected answer: Simple.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'you *should* read more,' is it a strong command or a suggestion?" (Expected answer: A suggestion.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, conversational dialogue (1-2 minutes) between two teenagers discussing their recent experiences with a "digital detox" or "tech-free challenge." The dialogue uses the target grammar (modals, comparatives, present perfect with 'since'/'for') and vocabulary ("benefits," "reduce," "interact," "distracted," "focused").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Discuss the positive and negative aspects of technology in a guided classroom debate, proposing solutions for overuse.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are the teenagers discussing in the dialogue?" (Expected: The pros and cons of using less technology, or tech-free activities.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> Students listen to the dialogue again and identify if statements are true or false. Examples: "One teen has *disconnected* from *social media* *since* last week." "They think *interacting* with friends face-to-face is *more meaningful* than online."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match problems (e.g., feeling *distracted*) with proposed *solutions* from the dialogue (e.g., *reduce* screen time).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Modals ("can," "could," "should") for ability and suggestions; Comparatives ("easier," "more difficult"); Present perfect with "since" and "for"; Vocabulary: "technology," "cellphone," "internet," "device," "social media," "solution," "benefits," "use," "reduce," "disconnect," "interact," "save," "helpful," "meaningful," "connected," "relaxed," "distracted," "focused," "often," "usually," "actively." Pragmatic functions: Giving opinions, discussing pros and cons, offering suggestions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> Students work individually to complete sentences with appropriate modals (can/could/should) or comparatives (easier/more difficult) and a verb from a word bank. Example: "To *reduce* screen time, you ___ try a new hobby." (should/could) "Reading books is ___ (easy) than playing games for me to feel *relaxed*." (easier) "I ___ (save) time *for* my hobbies *since* I *disconnected* from *social media*." (have saved, disconnected – *Teacher monitors for accurate pronunciation of -ed endings like /d/ in 'saved' and /ɪd/ in 'disconnected'*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Paired Q&A:</b> In pairs, students use provided question prompts to ask and answer questions about their habits, incorporating target grammar and vocabulary.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt 1: "What tech-free activities *can* you do to feel more *focused*?" (Response: "I *can* read or play sports. I think it's *easier* for me to be *focused* then.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt 2: "What *should* people do to *reduce* *social media* use?" (Response: "They *should* set specific hours. It's *more difficult* to stop if you *use* your *device* all day.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt 3: "How *have* you *interacted* with friends *since* you tried to *reduce* *cellphone* *use*?" (Response: "I *have interacted* with them face-to-face *more often* *since* I *saved* my *internet* data.") (*Teacher provides feedback on pronunciation of /θ/, /ð/ and -ed endings.*)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Tech Habits Survey":</b> Students interview their partner using a short survey sheet about their daily *technology* *use* and *tech-free* habits. They must formulate questions and answers using the target language. For example: "How *often* do you *use* your *cellphone* for non-school *benefits*?" "What are the *benefits* of *disconnecting* from the *internet* for a few hours?" "What *solutions* *could* you suggest to a friend who feels *distracted* by their *device*?" They record their partner's answers, practicing active listening and asking follow-up questions.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Role-Play – "The Distracted Study Group Meeting."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into groups of 3-4. Each group is given a scenario: "You are a study group preparing for a big project, but one or more members are constantly *distracted* by their *cellphones* and *social media*."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students must collectively *negotiate solutions* to this challenge. They take on roles: the distracted student(s), the concerned student(s), and a group leader.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must discuss the problem, using phrases for *giving opinions* ("I think *technology* is useful, but it *can* be *distracting*."), *discussing pros and cons* ("Digital *technology* makes communication *easier*, but it *could* make our group work *more difficult*."), and *offering suggestions* ("You *could* try putting your *device* away during study time." "We *should* set a rule for *tech-free* hours during our meetings."). They must also refer to the *benefits* of being *focused* and *connected* to the task.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group presents their role-play for 2-3 minutes, demonstrating how they discuss the problem and come to a *solution*. Teacher reminds students to be mindful of their pronunciation, particularly /θ/ and /ð/ sounds in words they use, and accurate "-ed" endings in verbs.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Distracted Study Group Meeting" role-play in their groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially /θ/, /ð/ sounds and -ed verb endings).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., "distracted," "solution," "reduce," "benefits").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of modals ("can," "could," "should") for suggestions and ability.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of comparatives ("easier," "more difficult").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective negotiation of solutions, asking and responding to questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and active interaction within the group.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in explaining routine tasks or offering suggestions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I successfully used these words/phrases to offer a *solution*: ___________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea I *could* try to *reduce* screen time *since* today's lesson is: __________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of negotiating solutions was *easier* or *more difficult* for you today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How *can* you apply the suggestions discussed today to your own *technology* *use*?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., Group role-plays encouraged active participation, pronunciation practice was integrated effectively).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with expressing opinions or offering suggestions? (e.g., Provide more sentence frames, review specific modals).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How will this lesson connect to the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition)? (This speaking practice of giving opinions and suggesting solutions will provide students with the necessary vocabulary and sentence structures to write a descriptive paragraph or short essay about their own tech-free routines or proposed solutions for tech overuse in the next lesson.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>